PYAsUKP: Yet Another solver (for the) Unbounded Knapsack Problem     

Note: This library is currently under development.

0. What is PYAsUKP ?

This is a solver for the Unbounded Knapsack Problems. It combines
branch and bound and dynamic programming approaches together with
the use of tight bounds.
Unbounded Knapsack Problems can be formulated as:
 maximize the value sum(pi xi) for i in 1..n
 subject to the constraintx sum(wi xi) <= c,
 and xi positive integers.
 

The current version address the cases when all pi wi and c
are positive integers.

1. In what language is written PYAsUKP?

It is written in Objective Caml: http://caml.inria.fr 

2. What does provide this library
    Obviously, an ukp solver
    but also: a data generator able to generate UKP data set according
              to formulae founded in the literature

3. How to use pyasukp?
  3.a As a standalone program
      Compile the native code version by make optt or the bytecode version
      by make bct, this should produce respectively pyasukpt and pyasukpbct 

      To obtain a light documentation 
  3.b As an ocaml function called in your ocaml program

  3.c As a C function.
    While written in ocaml, and thanks to the ability of the ocaml system to
    be interfaced with C, two C functions are provided:

      	pyasukp(warray,parray,c,n,isol,nbsol,capsol,profsol,k) returns in
	nbsol, capsol and profsol the result of the resolution of
   	knapsack defined with the arrays of weights and profits warray, parray
   	and the capacity c. The length of each array is (n+1).  
	nbsol is an array of size k such that nbsol[i] is the number of item isol[i] 
	to put in the knapsack to obtain the optimal solution capsol,profsol.

	void  pyasukp(long * warray, long * parray, int n, int c, long ** isol,
                   long ** nbsol, long * capsol, long * profsol, int * k)

	pyasukp_from_file is to use if data are in the file filename instead
        of arrays.

	void  pyasukp_from_file(char * filename, long ** isol,
        			long ** nbsol, long * capsol, long * profsol, int * k)

     You have to link your programs with the library pyasukp.a and the ocaml nums library.

     An example of use is given in the file main.c of the distribution. and below is
     how I link it on my linux box:
	gcc -o sukpc main.c pyasukp.a -lm -L/usr/lib/ocaml/3.08/ -lnums  -ldl